  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ハナダイコン」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%8F%E3%83%8A%E3%83%80%E3%82%A4%E3%82%B3%E3%83%B3",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 110966479,
      "text": "ハナダイコン は、アブラナ科の植物の一種。シベリアから西アジア・ヨーロッパにかけてが原産地で、欧米では、最も伝統のある園芸植物とされている。オオアラセイトウ もハナダイコンと呼ばれるが、別種である。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Hesperis matronalis」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hesperis_matronalis",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1353838499,
      "text": "Hesperis matronalis is an herbaceous flowering plant species in the family Brassicaceae. It has numerous common names, including dame's rocket, damask-violet, dame's-violet, dames-wort, dame's gilliflower, night-scented gilliflower, queen's gilliflower, rogue's gilliflower, sweet rocket, and mother-of-the-evening."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "cultivation": {
      "ja": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ハナダイコン」の節「栽培」",
        "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%8F%E3%83%8A%E3%83%80%E3%82%A4%E3%82%B3%E3%83%B3#%E6%A0%BD%E5%9F%B9",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 110966479,
        "heading": "栽培",
        "text": "秋分の頃に、畑に直まきする。覆土は浅くし、1mm位がよい。徐々に間引いて株間が25cmくらいになるようにする。日当たりと排水の良い所ならあまり土質は選ばないが、どちらかと言えば寒地向きの草花である。",
        "truncated": false
      },
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Hesperis matronalis」の節「Cultivation」",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hesperis_matronalis#Cultivation",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
        "revision": 1353838499,
        "heading": "Cultivation",
        "text": "Hesperis matronalis has been a cultivated species for a long time, and grows best in full sun to partial shade where soils are moist with good drainage. It is undemanding and self-seeds quickly, forming dense stands. Extensive monotypic stands of dame's rocket are visible from great distances; these dense collections of plants have the potential to crowd out native species when growing outside of cultivated areas.\nThe successful spread of dame's rocket in North America is attributed to its prolific seed production and because the seeds are often included in prepackaged \"wildflower seed\" mixes sold for \"naturalizing\". The plants typically produce a low-lying rosette of foliage the first year; in subsequent years, blooming and seed production occurs in tandem throughout the blooming season. This species is commonly found in roadside ditches, dumps and in open woodland settings, where it is noticed when in bloom. Although it makes an attractive, hardy garden plant, it has been found to be ecologically invasive in North America.\nHesperis matronalis is propagated by seeds, but desirable individuals, including the double-flowering forms, are propagated from cuttings or division of the clumps.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
